Subject: [PATCH] Make wrapper metadata serialization a direct LTO input

With GCC 16.2.1 and binutils 2.46.1, the wrapper link extracts the VPUMI37XX
network_metadata.cpp object from its archive after LTO symbol selection.
Its native references to ELF Logger, RuntimeError and a Hashtable destructor
then remain unresolved, although definitions exist in the LTO inputs.

The original link fails both serially and with a regular archive. Including
the same metadata object directly before archive scanning links successfully;
the map confirms its plugin references select ELF definitions at that stage.
Build this serialization source directly into the wrapper. The existing
archive remains available for other consumers; linker flags, warnings and
dependencies are unchanged. This avoids a global archive or optimization
change and does not depend on generated object-file paths.

Regression: the actual level_zero_wrapper target must link with its generated
Fedora RPM flags. The unmodified target fails on the three references above.

--- a/src/mlir_runtime/level_zero_wrapper/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/mlir_runtime/level_zero_wrapper/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -8,12 +8,15 @@
 add_library(${TARGET_NAME} SHARED
     level_zero_wrapper.h
     level_zero_wrapper.cpp
+    # Keep this object visible to GCC LTO before archive-member selection.
+    ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/vpux_compiler/src/dialect/VPUMI37XX/ops/network_metadata.cpp
 )
 if(WIN32)
     target_compile_definitions(${TARGET_NAME} PRIVATE NPU_I_API_DLL)
 endif()
 
 target_include_directories(${TARGET_NAME} PRIVATE
+    $<TARGET_PROPERTY:npu_compiler_dialect_vpumi37xx,INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES>
     ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/vpux_compiler/include
     ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/vpux_utils/include
     ${MLIR_INCLUDE_DIRS}
